// Brackets a lifetime. `@mount` runs when the node enters the tree, `@unmount`
// when it leaves, and the pair is what a component uses to hold something the
// program has to give back -- a subscription, a scheduled callback, a request
// in flight.
//
// It is placed, not declared: an effect is a node, so it sits where it belongs
// in the body and takes its lifetime from the tree around it. One inside an
// `if` lives as long as that branch does. A component places as many as it has
// things to hold, which is why this is a node rather than a hook on the
// declaration -- two brackets over different lifetimes cannot be one hook.
//
// `on` makes the bracket a keyed identity: while it holds the same value this
// is the same effect, and a different value is a different one, so the runtime
// ends the old lifetime and begins a new one -- `@unmount`, then `@mount`.
// Omitted, the bracket lives as long as the node does, which is what the
// parameter's default says: a key of a type with one value never changes.
//
// Each handler takes the value its own lifetime is keyed on. `@unmount` needs
// it because by the time teardown runs `on` already holds the next value, so a
// handler reading the cell would see what it is *not* tearing down; `@mount`
// takes it for symmetry, since the alternative is two handlers that differ for
// no reason a caller can see.
//
// **What a handler reads does not subscribe it.** A handler runs when its event
// fires, here as everywhere else in the language, and mount is an event. An
// effect that should run again when a value changes says so in `on`; nothing is
// inferred from the body. That is the whole difference from a computed, which is
// re-evaluated precisely because of what it reads.
// It belongs to no tree, which is what lets one bracket a lifetime inside a
// drawing or a menu as readily as inside a layout: an effect renders nothing,
// so it is no more a widget than it is a shape.
//
// **A key that does not compare is watched rather than identified.** A list, a
// map, or a struct holding one has no equality every target agrees on, so it
// cannot say when a lifetime ends: an effect keyed on one lives as long as its
// node, and it may not handle `@mount` or `@unmount`, which would run once and
// read as though they ran on every change. What it reports instead is each write
// to what `on` reads, which the compiler knows without comparing anything.
//
// `@update` is handed the value after a write. A list keyed on by its own cell
// -- a var, a prop, a field of either, never a computed list -- may also report
// how it changed: `@insert` for a push or an insert, `@remove` with the item
// removed, `@set` for an index assignment or a write inside an element, and
// `@clear`. A write no such handler describes, replacing the list whole among
// them, still reaches `@update`, so a program handling the changes has to handle
// `@update` too. They run after the handler that wrote, in the order the writes
// were made, each index read against the list as it stood at that write, and
// each item as that write left it: a later write in the same handler does not
// move it.
//
// For a key that does compare, `@update` runs on every write, including one of
// the value it already held, and before the bracket rekeys. No handler may write
// what `on` reads.
//
// `I` and `E` are what a list's changes are reported in -- its index and its
// element type -- and are bound from `T` rather than written.
#[_builtin(effect)]
component effect[T any = struct {}, I any = int, E any = dyn, F any](
    on T,
    @mount(key T),
    @unmount(key T),
    @update(value T),
    @insert(at I, item E),
    @set(at I, item E),
    @remove(at I, item E),
    @clear(),
) F {}
